  "docs": {
    "doc1_counterparty_risk.md": "# Counterparty Risk and Transaction Monitoring\n\nA Counterparty is any external party a bank transacts with \u2014 another bank, a corporate client, or a government entity. Every Transaction is linked to a Counterparty, and each Counterparty is assigned a Risk Rating (Low, Medium, High) based on factors such as credit history, jurisdiction, and industry sector.\n\nThe Transaction Monitoring System flags transactions for manual review when they breach predefined thresholds \u2014 for example, a large payment to a Counterparty rated High risk, or a transaction routed through a jurisdiction on a sanctions watchlist.\n\nWhen a Counterparty's Risk Rating changes (for example, following a credit downgrade), all open Transactions linked to that Counterparty are automatically re-evaluated under Credit Risk policy. This re-evaluation may trigger additional collateral requirements or a reduction in the Counterparty's approved exposure limit.\n\nCounterparty Risk is one of several categories monitored under the bank's broader Enterprise Risk Framework, alongside Market Risk and Liquidity Risk.\n",
    "doc2_credit_risk.md": "# Credit Risk\n\nCredit Risk is the risk that a Counterparty fails to meet its payment obligations on a Transaction. Banks manage Credit Risk by setting an Exposure Limit per Counterparty \u2014 the maximum amount the bank is willing to have outstanding with that party at any time.\n\nCredit Risk is measured using Probability of Default (PD), Loss Given Default (LGD), and Exposure at Default (EAD). These three figures combine to produce an Expected Loss estimate for each Counterparty relationship.\n\nUnder the Basel III regulatory framework, banks must hold a minimum level of Capital proportional to their Risk-Weighted Assets, which are calculated directly from Credit Risk exposures across all Counterparties. A Counterparty with a High Risk Rating contributes more heavily to Risk-Weighted Assets, increasing the Capital the bank must hold against it.\n\nCredit Risk sits within the Enterprise Risk Framework alongside Market Risk and Liquidity Risk, and is typically the largest single risk category for a commercial bank's Treasury Desk to manage.\n",
    "doc3_basel_iii.md": "# Basel III and Regulatory Capital\n\nBasel III is an international regulatory framework, developed by the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision, that sets minimum Capital and liquidity requirements for banks. It was introduced in response to weaknesses in bank capital regulation exposed by the 2008 financial crisis.\n\nBasel III requires banks to maintain a minimum Capital Adequacy Ratio, calculated as Capital divided by Risk-Weighted Assets. Risk-Weighted Assets are derived primarily from Credit Risk exposures, with additional weightings for Market Risk and Operational Risk.\n\nBasel III also introduced the Liquidity Coverage Ratio, which requires banks to hold enough high-quality liquid assets to survive a 30-day stress scenario. This requirement falls under Liquidity Risk management, typically owned by the Treasury Desk.\n\nBanks report Basel III compliance metrics to their national regulator on a periodic basis. A breach of the Capital Adequacy Ratio or Liquidity Coverage Ratio requires immediate escalation and a remediation plan, since regulators can restrict a bank's operations \u2014 including dividend payments and new Transaction approvals \u2014 until compliance is restored.\n",
    "doc4_treasury_liquidity.md": "# Treasury Desk and Liquidity Risk\n\nThe Treasury Desk is responsible for managing a bank's overall funding, liquidity position, and interest rate exposure. It ensures the bank always holds enough cash and high-quality liquid assets to meet obligations as they fall due \u2014 this is the core of Liquidity Risk management.\n\nLiquidity Risk arises when a bank cannot meet short-term obligations without incurring unacceptable losses, for example if too many Counterparties draw on committed credit lines at once, or if a large Transaction settlement falls on a day when liquid asset buffers are already low.\n\nThe Treasury Desk monitors the Liquidity Coverage Ratio daily, a metric mandated under Basel III, and maintains a buffer of high-quality liquid assets above the regulatory minimum. It also runs stress tests simulating scenarios such as a sudden Counterparty default or a market-wide funding freeze.\n\nLiquidity Risk is closely linked to Credit Risk: a wave of Counterparty defaults not only creates direct losses but can also drain the liquid asset buffers the Treasury Desk relies on, creating a Liquidity Risk event triggered by a Credit Risk event.\n"
  }
